//? Title: CIFAR-10: a reproducible image-classification study
//? Subtitle: Converged compact ResNet-18, held-out errors, probability calibration and interpretability
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Assigning an object category to a 32-by-32 colour image requires separating object shape from background, pose and severe loss of detail. This study examines all 60,000 CIFAR-10 images, trains a compact residual network from scratch on a fixed fitting partition, and evaluates the selected model on the complete official test set. It reports learning-rate selection, an explicit convergence check, uncertainty, class-specific errors and reproducible examples. Gradient explanations are checked numerically and compared with finite occlusions and a randomised-head control. All reported numbers are computed locally; trained weights, split IDs, logits and numerical explanation arrays accompany the report.
)
\toc

> Scope and study design

The target is the original ten-class CIFAR-10 task: one image in, one of ten mutually exclusive labels out. The objective is a sufficient, interpretable and reproducible classifier rather than a leaderboard claim. The chosen network retains all eight basic residual blocks of ResNet-18, with narrower channels and a small-image stem. It uses no pretrained weights, external images, ensembling or test-time augmentation.

All selection uses the official training partition. A seeded, exactly stratified split supplies 45,000 fitting images and 5,000 validation images. The validation partition chooses the learning rate and checkpoint, then fits one probability temperature. The 10,000 official test images are scored after these decisions are frozen. Because the same validation set serves these three purposes, its final score is a selection diagnostic, not an unbiased generalisation estimate.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/study_design.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle blue;
auto box = blue;
  box.width = 4;
  diagram.node("training", {0, 3}, "50,000 training images\nStratified split, fixed seed", box)
      .node("fit", {-3.2, 0}, "45,000 fitting images\nFitting RGB moments\nAugmentation + Adam", box)
      .node("validation", {3.2, 0}, "5,000 validation images\nRate, checkpoint, temperature", box)
      .node("model", {0, -3}, "Frozen ResNet-18 + BN\nFixed RGB moments\nand temperature T", box)
      .node("test", {-5.5, -3}, "10,000 test images\nScored after selection", box)
      .node("analysis", {5.5, -3}, "Errors and calibration\nGrad-CAM, IG, occlusion", box)
      .edge("training", "fit").edge("training", "validation")
      .edge("fit", "model").edge("validation", "model")
      .edge("test", "model").edge("model", "analysis");
  diagram.save_svg("build/study_design.svg");
