\image(figures/class_means.png)
// result_end
\caption(Class-mean images in label order, IDs 0–4 then 5–9. Blur reflects pose and location variation. Colour or background patterns suggest possible shortcuts; averages do not establish which features the classifier uses.)
\alttext(Ten average images show broad colour and position patterns without sharp object boundaries.)

The average tiles preserve blue backgrounds for airplanes and ships, greener backgrounds in several animal classes, and a central dark vehicle region for automobiles and trucks. These are visible label-associated image patterns. They motivate checking object-versus-context sensitivity, without showing that the fitted classifier actually uses a shortcut. Individual previews also show large changes in object scale and incomplete outlines at this resolution.

> Model and optimisation method

The network maps a standardised NHWC image to ten unconstrained logits. A 3-by-3 stride-one convolution produces 16 channels without an initial pooling layer. Four stages contain two basic residual blocks each, at widths 16, 32, 64 and 128. The first block of each later stage downsamples by two with a learned projection shortcut. Global average pooling feeds a biased linear classifier. Batch normalisation uses epsilon \(10^{-5}\) and retains 0.9 of each previous moving moment. Moving moments and learned parameters are checkpointed together.

This is an explicit four-stage small-image adaptation of [He et al., *Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition*|https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385], not the paper's three-stage CIFAR architecture. Narrowing the network gives a practical native CPU experiment while retaining the residual structure and enough capacity for this task. A best-in-class accuracy claim would require a broader architecture and augmentation comparison that this study does not perform.

For a basic block, the learnable branch is a composition of two normalised 3-by-3 convolutions, with ReLU after the first. Its output is

$$h_{l+1}=\operatorname{ReLU}\{P_lh_l+F_l(h_l;\theta_l)\}.$$
\alttext(The next feature map is the rectified sum of a matching or projected shortcut and a learned residual branch.)

The fitting objective averages sparse softmax cross entropy over each minibatch and adds a kernel-only squared penalty:

$$L(\theta)=\frac1B\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left[-z_{i,y_i}+\log\sum_{c=1}^{10}e^{z_{i,c}}\right]+\frac{10^{-4}}2\sum_{W:\,\dim W\ge2}\lVert W\rVert_F^2.$$
\alttext(The objective is mean negative log likelihood plus one half times 0.0001 times the sum of squared convolution and classifier kernels.)

Adam uses beta1 0.9, beta2 0.999 and epsilon 1e-8, with minibatches of 64. Bias and BN parameters are excluded from the explicit L2 term. Each fitting image receives an independent random crop after four-pixel raw-black padding and an independent horizontal flip with probability one half; RGB standardisation follows augmentation. Validation uses original images and frozen BN moments. There is no label smoothing, mixup, CutMix, dropout or test-time augmentation.

Two six-epoch pilots compare peak rates 0.001 and 0.003. Both reset the initial parameters, BN moments, minibatch seed and epoch-specific augmentation streams; each warms up over its first epoch. The candidate with the lowest observed validation NLL is continued from its sixth-epoch state, including Adam slots and batch-order state. Its rate follows a cosine from the selected peak at epoch 7 to 0.00001 at epoch 80. Every epoch is evaluated, and the smallest raw validation NLL retains a paired weight/BN candidate. After the plateau check, validation accuracy selects between that candidate and the converged endpoint; calibrated validation NLL breaks exact accuracy ties. The losing pilot is never evaluated on test data.

Training continues at the nonzero floor rate for ten epochs. Convergence is an operational plateau: the absolute difference between the last five-epoch mean and preceding five-epoch mean must be below 0.005 nats for both augmented fitting NLL and validation NLL. Ten-epoch extensions are allowed up to epoch 120; failure to meet the criterion makes the training program fail. This documents stability of this optimisation schedule, not global optimality or the absence of overfitting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/cifar10_study.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/classification_analysis.hpp>
#include <map>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace s = n::study;
  n::set_num_threads(2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  n::ParameterStore p(17), state(17);
  const auto        cfg = s::cifar10_resnet_config();
  n::Module         model(
      [&](const n::Tensor& image) {
        const auto out      = n::models::ResNet(p, state, image, cfg, false);
        auto       previous = image;
        n::model_stage("Stem", image, out.stem);
        previous = out.stem;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
          n::model_stage("Stage " + std::to_string(i + 1), previous, out.stages[i]);
          previous = out.stages[i];
        }
        return out.logits;
      },
      "CIFAR-10 compact ResNet-18");
  n::ModelPlotOptions options;
  options.style                 = n::ModelPlotStyle::Flow;
  options.detail                = n::ModelPlotDetail::Blocks;
  options.labels                = {.type = n::ModelPlotLabel::NameAndShape, .size = 12, .wrap = 24};
  options.spacing               = {.layer = 24, .lane = 12, .label = 8, .margin = 20};
  options.sizing.spatial_size   = 96;
  options.sizing.channel_size   = 42;
  options.sizing.minimum_extent = 1;
  const auto diagram            = model.plot(n::zeros({1, 32, 32, 3}), options);
  diagram.save_svg("build/architecture.svg");
  std::print(
      "| Quantity | Executed value |\n|---|---:|\n| Learned scalars | {} |\n| BN moving scalars | "
      "{} |\n| Named plotted nodes | {} |\n",
      p.count(),
      state.count(),
      diagram.nodes().size());
}
```
