> Results, interpretation and limitations

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/cifar10_study.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/classification_analysis.hpp>
#include <map>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  namespace st = n::study;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> a, e;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("artifacts/evaluation.npz"))
    a[k] = v;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("artifacts/explanations.npz"))
    e[k] = v;
  const auto run    = n::read_csv("artifacts/training_summary.tsv", nullptr, true, '\t');
  const auto raw    = st::classification_diagnostics(a.at("test_logits"), a.at("test_labels"));
  const auto scaled = st::classification_diagnostics(
      a.at("test_logits"), a.at("test_labels"), a.at("temperature").item());
  const auto matrix = n::ConfusionMatrix::from_counts(a.at("confusion"));
  const auto errors = 10000 - std::llround(10000 * matrix.accuracy());
  int        from = 0, to = 1, weak = 0, strong = 0;
  uint64_t   largest = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    if (matrix.per_class(i).recall < matrix.per_class(weak).recall)
      weak = i;
    if (matrix.per_class(i).recall > matrix.per_class(strong).recall)
      strong = i;
    for (int j = 0; j < 10; ++j)
      if (i != j && matrix.cells()[i][j] > largest) {
        largest = matrix.cells()[i][j];
        from    = i;
        to      = j;
      }
  }
  const auto interval = dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(10000 - errors, 10000);
  std::print(
      "The selected checkpoint at epoch {:.0f} achieves {:.2f}% official-test accuracy "
      "(conditional Wilson 95% interval {:.2f}%–{:.2f}%), leaving {} errors in 10000 images. "
      "Macro F1 is {:.4f}. The largest directed confusion is {} → {} ({} images). "
      "Recall is lowest for {} ({:.1f}%) and highest for {} ({:.1f}%).\n\n",
      run.at({0, 2}),
      100 * matrix.accuracy(),
      100 * interval.conf_int_lower,
      100 * interval.conf_int_upper,
      errors,
      matrix.macro().f1,
      st::cifar10_classes[from],
      st::cifar10_classes[to],
      largest,
      st::cifar10_classes[weak],
      100 * matrix.per_class(weak).recall,
      st::cifar10_classes[strong],
      100 * matrix.per_class(strong).recall);
  std::print(
      "Validation-only temperature scaling uses T={:.4f}. On test data, NLL changes from "
      "{:.5f} to {:.5f}, Brier score from {:.5f} to {:.5f}, and 15-bin ECE from {:.5f} to {:.5f}. "
      "Top-one accuracy is unchanged. Probability calibration {} test NLL in this run; "
      "this is an observed held-out result, not a guarantee for other data.\n\n",
      a.at("temperature").item(),
      raw.nll,
      scaled.nll,
      raw.brier,
      scaled.brier,
      raw.ece,
      scaled.ece,
      scaled.nll < raw.nll ? "improves" : "does not improve");
  const double residual = n::max(n::abs(e.at("completeness512"))).item();
  const double scaled_error =
      n::max(n::abs(e.at("completeness512")) / (1 + n::abs(e.at("score_difference")))).item();
  const double top    = n::mean(e.at("ig_top25_drop")).item(),
               random = n::mean(e.at("random25_drop")).item();
  std::print(
      "For the eight displayed cases, the largest absolute 512-node IG completeness residual is "
      "{:.5f} logit units; the largest residual divided by one plus the absolute logit difference "
      "is {:.5f}. Mean target-logit drops after IG-ranked and random 25% deletion are {:.4f} "
      "and {:.4f}, respectively. {} These selected-case diagnostics cannot establish population "
      "faithfulness, and quadrature residuals need not decrease monotonically with node count.\n",
      residual,
      scaled_error,
      top,
      random,
      top > random
          ? "IG-ranked deletion reduces the target score more on average in this small comparison."
          : "IG-ranked deletion does not outperform the random control on average in this small "
            "comparison.");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The selected checkpoint at epoch 90 achieves 90.21% official-test accuracy (conditional Wilson 95% interval 89.61%–90.78%), leaving 979 errors in 10000 images. Macro F1 is 0.9021. The largest directed confusion is cat → dog (90 images). Recall is lowest for cat (79.5%) and highest for automobile (96.1%).

Validation-only temperature scaling uses T=2.2789. On test data, NLL changes from 0.44436 to 0.30200, Brier score from 0.15832 to 0.14321, and 15-bin ECE from 0.06068 to 0.00571. Top-one accuracy is unchanged. Probability calibration improves test NLL in this run; this is an observed held-out result, not a guarantee for other data.

For the eight displayed cases, the largest absolute 512-node IG completeness residual is 0.06411 logit units; the largest residual divided by one plus the absolute logit difference is 0.00490. Mean target-logit drops after IG-ranked and random 25% deletion are 10.6033 and 9.4792, respectively. IG-ranked deletion reduces the target score more on average in this small comparison. These selected-case diagnostics cannot establish population faithfulness, and quadrature residuals need not decrease monotonically with node count.
// result_end


The stratified validation design prevents row-ID overlap between fitting and validation; all official test decisions are retained. The exact-image audit quantifies pixel-identical overlap, which is distinct from row-ID overlap. It does not remove duplicates or measure dependence between visually similar observations. Shared fitting/validation images, if present in the audit, make the selection score more optimistic. Image-level Wilson intervals therefore provide only a conditional sampling summary. One initialisation seed and two learning-rate candidates do not establish variance across training runs or an architecture ranking. The class labels are the benchmark annotations: no human relabelling of ambiguous examples was performed.

Explanations should be read as model diagnostics. Grad-CAM is spatially coarse, IG depends on its baseline and numerical quadrature, and patch replacement can create unnatural images. Per-image colour normalisation removes absolute magnitude. Agreement among maps can be informative, but it does not establish causality, object segmentation or robustness to distribution shift. The current report measures the original clean test task; it does not claim adversarial robustness, corruption robustness or performance on contemporary photographs.

> Reproducibility and retained artifacts

Run this report with the normal repository C++ tooling and a matching working-tree `pkg-config` path:

```
PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/absolute/path/to/build/cifar10-report/pc ./docs/mepml/examples/datasets/cifar10/run.sh
```

The `.pc` file must point at matching checkout headers and a built native library, as in the repository's report workflow. For an installed build, `nix develop .#cpp` supplies the normal package configuration. `./run.sh --train` executes both pilots and training to the explicit convergence criterion; it can take substantially longer on other CPUs. Ordinary builds use the included converged training artifacts, recompute inference and explanations, then regenerate every result and figure. There is no Python data-preparation dependency. Exact retraining across compilers, standard libraries or BLAS backends is not guaranteed bit for bit; the retained checkpoint and logits define this measured run.

The `artifacts/` directory retains selected parameters, corresponding BN moments, preprocessing moments, original fitting/validation row IDs, both pilot histories, the selected-run epoch history, the convergence summary, both checkpoint candidates and their validation-only comparison, complete validation and test logits, prediction examples and raw interpretability arrays. `dataset.sha256` fingerprints the six input files; `checksums.sha256` fingerprints retained numerical artifacts. The runtime manifest records compiler, library digest and source fingerprints because a revision identifier alone cannot describe a dirty working tree. Figure sources remain editable. Large transient checkpoints, compiled programs and rendering logs stay under ignored `build/` directories.

An optional independent development oracle, `scripts/check_cifar10_study.py`, uses an existing NumPy/PyTorch environment to compare every official-test logit and confusion entry, the proper probability scores, Grad-CAM and 512-node IG. It does not train or acquire data and is not part of report execution. The native offline tests are `run_cifar10_study_tests`; their analytic fixtures verify proper scores, temperature behaviour, confidence-one binning and input validation.

All 24 C++ programs and two shell blocks executed successfully; the unlabelled reproduction-command example is intentionally not executed. Fifteen retained figures were reviewed, with SVGs rendered through Inkscape at 1100 pixels. Nine native tests, twelve compiled header examples, five clangd hovers and all 35 affected documentation definitions pass. An independent NumPy/PyTorch check agrees on all 10000 test predictions and every confusion count: maximum test-logit difference 1.53e-5, probability-score difference 4.14e-13, fitted-temperature difference 5.11e-9, Grad-CAM difference 1.20e-6, and 512-node IG RMSE 2.36e-5. These floating comparisons allow differences at piecewise-linear activation boundaries. Markdown and website HTML exports are checked separately. The independent-oracle figures describe the retained run; repeat that optional check after retraining. Full local evidence is under `build/cifar10-report/`.
